1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,119
At the college fest, Aarav
taps a tiny microphone.

2
00:00:03,419 --> 00:00:06,333
The huge speakers boom
across the whole ground.

3
00:00:06,633 --> 00:00:10,114
How can such a tiny signal
control such a big current?

4
00:00:10,614 --> 00:00:12,659
The secret is a transistor.

5
00:00:12,659 --> 00:00:15,802
Its full name is bipolar
junction transistor.

6
00:00:16,102 --> 00:00:19,280
Think of a big water pipe
with a small control tap.

7
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:23,075
A gentle turn of the small
tap controls the big flow.

8
00:00:23,375 --> 00:00:26,929
In a transistor, a small
current controls a big current.

9
00:00:27,529 --> 00:00:28,669
Let's build one.

10
00:00:28,669 --> 00:00:31,449
A transistor is a
sandwich of three layers.

11
00:00:31,749 --> 00:00:33,556
The first layer is the emitter.

12
00:00:33,556 --> 00:00:35,447
It sends out the charge carriers.

13
00:00:35,797 --> 00:00:37,849
The middle layer is the base.

14
00:00:37,849 --> 00:00:39,383
It controls the flow.

15
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:41,520
The last layer is the collector.

16
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,927
It collects the carriers.

17
00:00:43,277 --> 00:00:45,699
Where two layers meet, we get a junction.

18
00:00:45,699 --> 00:00:46,826
So there are two.

19
00:00:47,176 --> 00:00:50,552
The emitter base junction, and
the collector base junction.

20
00:00:51,152 --> 00:00:53,220
Each layer is built for its job.

21
00:00:53,570 --> 00:00:55,313
The emitter is heavily doped.

22
00:00:55,313 --> 00:00:57,291
It has lots of carriers to send.

23
00:00:57,641 --> 00:01:00,260
The base is very thin and lightly doped.

24
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:02,953
So very few carriers get lost inside it.

25
00:01:03,303 --> 00:01:05,415
The collector is the largest layer.

26
00:01:05,415 --> 00:01:07,034
It must get rid of heat.

27
00:01:07,634 --> 00:01:09,615
There are two kinds of transistor.

28
00:01:09,915 --> 00:01:13,438
NPN has a thin p base
between two n layers.

29
00:01:13,788 --> 00:01:17,387
PNP has a thin n base
between two p layers.

30
00:01:17,737 --> 00:01:20,291
In the symbol, an arrow
sits on the emitter.

31
00:01:20,641 --> 00:01:23,419
It points the way
conventional current flows.

32
00:01:23,769 --> 00:01:26,133
For NPN, the arrow points out.

33
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:28,050
For PNP, it points in.

34
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:31,554
A handy trick: NPN means not pointing in.

35
00:01:32,154 --> 00:01:35,682
Now let's look inside an
NPN transistor at work.

36
00:01:35,982 --> 00:01:38,744
We bias the two junctions
in different ways.

37
00:01:39,094 --> 00:01:41,943
The emitter base junction
is forward biased.

38
00:01:41,943 --> 00:01:43,273
Its gate is open.

39
00:01:43,623 --> 00:01:46,424
The collector base
junction is reverse biased.

40
00:01:46,424 --> 00:01:48,066
It pulls carriers across.

41
00:01:48,666 --> 00:01:51,280
Electrons rush from the
emitter into the thin base.

42
00:01:51,630 --> 00:01:55,308
Picture runners crossing a
thin, almost empty field.

43
00:01:55,658 --> 00:01:58,775
A few runners meet holes
in the base and pair up.

44
00:01:59,125 --> 00:02:01,111
This is called recombination.

45
00:02:01,111 --> 00:02:03,855
It makes the small base current, IB.

46
00:02:04,205 --> 00:02:07,323
Most runners shoot straight
across into the collector.

47
00:02:07,673 --> 00:02:10,606
They make the big collector current, IC.

48
00:02:11,206 --> 00:02:12,687
Now on the breadboard.

49
00:02:12,687 --> 00:02:14,700
Watch the meters and the scope.

50
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:17,082
Raise the base current a little.

51
00:02:17,082 --> 00:02:19,207
The collector current rises a lot.

52
00:02:19,557 --> 00:02:21,566
Now let the base current wiggle.

53
00:02:21,566 --> 00:02:24,030
The collector current wiggles much bigger.

54
00:02:24,630 --> 00:02:25,582
Now the rule.

55
00:02:25,582 --> 00:02:27,972
Every electron enters through the emitter.

56
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:31,880
So the emitter current,
IE, is the biggest current.

57
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:36,643
It splits into two parts: the base
current and the collector current.

58
00:02:37,343 --> 00:02:39,804
So IE equals IB plus IC.

59
00:02:40,504 --> 00:02:43,722
This is Kirchhoff's current
law, used on the transistor.

60
00:02:44,422 --> 00:02:46,311
The base current is tiny.

61
00:02:46,311 --> 00:02:48,714
So IC is almost equal to IE.

62
00:02:49,314 --> 00:02:52,595
It is called bipolar because both
electrons and holes take part.

63
00:02:53,195 --> 00:02:56,652
A thin base lets a small
current control a big one.

64
00:02:57,452 --> 00:02:58,703
Back to the fest.

65
00:02:58,703 --> 00:03:01,636
The microphone sends a
tiny signal to the base.

66
00:03:01,986 --> 00:03:05,589
The collector current copies it,
much bigger, and the speakers boom.

67
00:03:06,189 --> 00:03:07,089
Worked example.

68
00:03:07,075 --> 00:03:09,795
A transistor has an emitter
current of ten milliamps.

69
00:03:10,145 --> 00:03:12,561
The base current is zero
point two milliamps.

70
00:03:12,561 --> 00:03:14,053
Find the collector current.

71
00:03:14,403 --> 00:03:16,740
IC equals IE minus IB.

72
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:20,204
Ten minus zero point two is
nine point eight milliamps.

73
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:24,774
So ninety eight percent of the
emitter current reaches the collector.

74
00:03:25,424 --> 00:03:26,886
Now, the exam trap.

75
00:03:27,236 --> 00:03:30,725
Many students think the base is
a thick, heavily doped layer.

76
00:03:31,075 --> 00:03:32,407
It is the opposite.

77
00:03:32,407 --> 00:03:35,360
The base is the thinnest
and most lightly doped.

78
00:03:36,010 --> 00:03:39,619
And the collector, not the
emitter, is the largest layer.

79
00:03:40,269 --> 00:03:41,604
Where is it asked?

80
00:03:41,604 --> 00:03:44,437
PUC and ISC board papers ask this often.

81
00:03:44,787 --> 00:03:49,015
Draw both structures and symbols,
then explain transistor action.

82
00:03:49,665 --> 00:03:50,565
Try this.

83
00:03:50,355 --> 00:03:53,416
In a transistor, the emitter
current is five milliamps.

84
00:03:53,416 --> 00:03:56,226
Two percent of the electrons
recombine in the base.

85
00:03:56,226 --> 00:03:57,950
What is the collector current?

86
00:04:03,350 --> 00:04:04,250
A.

87
00:04:03,575 --> 00:04:04,557
Four point nine milliamps.

88
00:04:04,907 --> 00:04:07,279
The base current is two
percent of five milliamps.

89
00:04:07,629 --> 00:04:09,255
That is zero point one milliamps.

90
00:04:09,605 --> 00:04:11,932
IC equals five minus zero point one.

91
00:04:11,932 --> 00:04:14,016
That is four point nine milliamps.

92
00:04:14,666 --> 00:04:16,212
Screenshot this card.

93
00:04:19,412 --> 00:04:22,683
Next, the common emitter
setup, alpha and beta.

94
00:04:22,683 --> 00:04:25,037
How much does a transistor amplify?
